<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to BlockML's unifying Everything-is-a-Block principle

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.EverythingIsABlockPage-->
<docs:EverythingIsABlockPage xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to BlockML's unifying Everything-is-a-Block principle
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: the Everything-is-a-Block model, single inheritance root, and why one grammar covers
    domain and framework types, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.EverythingIsABlock and
    org.blockml.bml.core.Block. Body content is authored in a03 from those sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:EverythingIsABlock" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — one root, one parser, one TypeRegistry bootstrap path.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Block" relation="Requires">
      Sole inheritance root every BlockML concept ultimately extends.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:CoreUseCases" relation="SeeAlso">
      What the principle is used for.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:BlockAndInstance" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — definition versus instance.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Everything Is a Block</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <!--1. Lead-->
        <dpage:Section heading="One idea underlies everything">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Every concept BlockML's grammar can express — a domain type, a value type, a
language, or a piece of documentation — is a Block, described the same way and traced
back to the same root.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--2. Single root-->
        <dpage:Section heading="A single root, one parser, one TypeRegistry">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Every Block ultimately extends the same sole inheritance root, is read by the
same parser, and is registered through the same bootstrap path, so branch-specific
behavior comes from that root's chain of specialization rather than from separate
parallel systems. This one root, one parser, one registry model applies uniformly,
whatever kind of Block is being described.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--3. Same grammar, many kinds-->
        <dpage:Section heading="The same grammar covers domain and framework concepts">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Several very different-looking things in BlockML all turn out to be Blocks
under this one principle.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="A composable domain type describing something in a modeled world is a Block." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A value type that describes the nature of a value, such as an integer or a size, is a Block." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="An implementation language binding used to express behavior is a Block." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="The definitions that describe a Block's own structure are themselves Blocks." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--4. Authoring ontology vs compiler internals-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Authoring ontology versus compiler internals">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Treating everything as a named, identifiable Block with its own place in a
chain of specialization is a property of the authoring model, not a requirement on
how the compiler must represent things internally. The authoring ontology gives
every Block a fully qualified name and a chain of specialization, while compiler
internals remain free to use whatever representation is convenient.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--5. Looking ahead-->
        <dpage:Section heading="What this principle sets up">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Because every concept is a Block, learning how one Block is defined, named,
and referenced is enough to start reading any part of a BlockML model.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--6. Continuation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with a single Block">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Blocks" summary="Learn how a single Block is defined, named, and referenced.">
                  <target>docs:Blocks</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:EverythingIsABlockPage>
